 Vite asset filenames identify their content, so immutable caching avoids revalidation round trips for lazy chunks over remote connections. Service workers need short-lived responses so clients, including iOS, can discover updates.
 
+### Why HTML documents are validated by their bytes and not by file stats
+
+An HTML document in the dist root (`index.html`, `mobile.html`, `mini-chat.html`, and `index.html` again as the SPA fallback) names its build's hashed assets, which clients hold as immutable, so one stale document keeps a whole old build running. `express.static` validates by size and mtime. Nix stamps every build output with the same mtime, so two builds whose documents had the same size sent the same ETag, and a new server answered 304 to a browser holding the old document. The static routes read each document and send it with `res.send`, whose ETag is a hash of the bytes, with `Cache-Control: no-cache` and no `Last-Modified`. `express.static` runs with `index: false` so `/` reaches the SPA fallback and gets the same treatment. Clients revalidate on every load and still get 304 while the build is unchanged. The document is read on every request because a hash taken at startup goes stale when the dist directory is rebuilt in place.
+
 ### Why isolated exceptions keep serving and not always stop the host
 
 A server embedded in Desktop or launched manually may have no supervisor to restart it after a stray socket exception. It logs isolated exceptions and keeps serving; a sustained exception storm triggers shutdown because continued operation is no longer reliable.
